/// A constructor invoked on an existing instance yields nil.
///
/// Calling X.Init(5) on a live instance re-runs the constructor over it, which
/// both processors do.  They disagree about the RESULT: the interpreter hands
/// back the instance, compiled code hands back nil.
///
/// ⚠️ The specification chooses nil, against the normative half, and the reason
/// is that the alternative contradicts a rule stated two paragraphs earlier: a
/// constructor may not return a value, and 'Exit this' inside one is refused.
/// A construct that cannot return a value but does return one when called a
/// different way is two rules where there should be one.  The interpreter's
/// answer is inherited from Lox, where an initializer returns 'this' so that
/// 'new Foo()' has something to evaluate to -- a reason that does not apply
/// here, since construction is a separate path with its own result.
///
/// Re-invocation itself is deliberate and Turbo Pascal does the same: there
/// P.Init(...) on an allocated object is how construction happens at all.
///
/// Fails with: Assertion 'left = right' failed.  Expected 'nil' but got 'W instance'.
///
class W;
var
    N : Integer := 0;

begin
    constructor Init (N : Integer);
    begin
        this.N := N;
    end
end

test 'A constructor invoked on an instance yields nil';
begin
    var Made := W (1);
    AssertEqual (1, Made.N);

    var Result := Made.Init (5);

    AssertEqual (5, Made.N);
    AssertEqual (Str ('nil'), Str (Result));
end
